  "title": "The falls of Rome and Constantinople",
  "hook": "AD 476 ended the western court; Constantinople followed a much longer, interrupted history.",
  "updated": "2026-10-14",
  "notes": "Historical locations are approximate city/site anchors, not surveyed battle sites or territorial polygons. No modern state is used as a proxy for Roman sovereignty. Events follow cited ancient accounts and institutional histories; ancient authors are not neutral eyewitnesses to every event. BC dates use astronomical years internally. Met Roman Empire for AD 410 and 476; Met Byzantium and Byzantium: Faith and Power for 1204, 1261 and 1453. AD 476 marks western deposition and eastern continuity, not a boundary snapshot. Western deposition is conventionally associated with Ravenna; Roman institutions survived it.",
